{
  "topic": "insulin",
  "ttsScript": "In 1923, three scientists sold the insulin patent for one dollar each. They believed no one should profit from a lifesaving medicine. That patent was meant to keep insulin cheap forever. Today, a single vial costs over three hundred dollars in the United States. The same drug. The same molecule. Drug companies found a loophole. They make tiny changes to the formula. Just enough to file a new patent. They call it innovation. Patients call it survival. One in four Americans with diabetes rations their insulin. Some have died. Three men sold their discovery for one dollar. Corporations turned it into a thirty billion dollar industry.",
